The '''History of Sarta (1337–1530)''' covers the period from the beginning of the [[Two Centuries War]] to the [[Convention of Paga]] in 1530. It was a period of recurring conflict, state consolidation, urban growth, and maritime expansion. Over these two centuries, Sarta developed a more self-conscious political identity, expanded its institutions of learning, and deepened the divide between its coastal and mountainous regions.

== Background ==

The period began in the aftermath of the [[Great Catholic Schism]], when the division of Catholic authority in Illypnia hardened into the [[Two Centuries War]]. The war was fought between the papal states, centred on [[Volisania]], and the Zamarran states led from [[Zamarra]] on [[Lake Zamarra]]. In Sarta, the conflict became part of the kingdom's political life rather than a separate frontier episode. Sartan rulers, nobles, and church leaders were drawn into a wider confessional struggle that shaped diplomacy, military service, and public identity.

The war also altered the balance between Sarta and its neighbours. [[Kingdom of Jacan|Jacan]] remained an important southern partner, while the rivalry with [[Kingdom of Kastruneto|Kastruneto]] defined the western frontier of the conflict. Sarta's role in the papal coalition placed it in a position of close involvement with the war, even when the main fighting was concentrated elsewhere.

== The Two Centuries War ==

The [[Two Centuries War]] began in 1337 and continued in changing forms until 1530. It was not a single continuous campaign but a long series of wars, truces, raids, and frontier offensives. The earliest and most sustained fighting took place in the valleys of the [[Rio Grande]] and [[Rio Verde]], especially in the border zone between Sarta and Kastruneto. Control of river corridors, fortified towns, and mountain passes mattered as much as control of individual cities.

The war changed Sarta's relationship with the wider region. The kingdom contributed troops, ships, and revenue to the papal side, while Volisania remained farther from the front and suffered less direct damage. This difference contributed to growing resentment in Sarta, especially among nobles, merchants, and officials who bore the costs of war without holding equivalent authority in the papal order. Over time, that resentment became a durable feature of Sartan political memory.

== State and society ==

The pressures of war encouraged the crown to strengthen administration. Kings relied more heavily on taxation, record-keeping, and service obligations, and the urban population grew in response to these demands. Merchants, clerks, notaries, shipwrights, teachers, and church administrators formed a larger urban middle stratum than had existed in earlier periods. Their influence increased in port towns and market centres, where war logistics and trade revenues intersected.

The crown also issued edicts to support higher learning. Universities were founded or reorganised to train jurists, clerics, physicians, and administrators. These institutions helped standardise legal practice and church learning, and they provided a setting in which educated Sartan identity could develop through theology, law, and historical writing. Over time, university culture became one of the main supports of elite Sartan self-definition.

== Sartan identity ==

During this period, Sartan identity became more explicit in political and cultural writing. Earlier Sartan-speaking communities had shared language and related elite traditions, but the long war encouraged rulers and writers to define Sarta as a distinct historical and religious community. Chronicles, sermons, legal texts, and royal correspondence increasingly treated the kingdom as a single political people rather than a loose collection of highland and lowland polities.

This identity was shaped by war but also by institutions. Church schools, universities, and royal administration all helped circulate common forms of language and status. The result was not complete uniformity, but a stronger sense that a Sartan subject belonged to a common kingdom with a shared past.

== Coastal and mountain Sarta ==

The long war sharpened differences between western and eastern Sarta. The western and coastal regions remained more connected to shipping, trade, and outside influence. They developed stronger links to Jacan and to the maritime world of the southern coasts. Coastal towns tended to be more commercially oriented and more exposed to foreign ideas.

The eastern and mountainous regions developed differently. They were more strongly tied to fortresses, inland routes, and defensive warfare. Mountain communities and inland nobles often maintained a more conservative political outlook and a stronger attachment to older forms of authority. The divide was not absolute, but it became increasingly visible in social and political life.

== Relations with Volisania ==

Sarta's relationship with [[Volisania]] grew more strained over the course of the war. The kingdom continued to support the papal side, but the burden of fighting increasingly fell on Sarta's own population. By contrast, Volisania was farther from the front and less exposed to the destruction of repeated campaigning. This asymmetry made the alliance feel less equal from a Sartan perspective.

By the later phases of the war, Sartan writers and officials increasingly described Volisania as an ally that benefited disproportionately from Sartan service. That sentiment did not prevent continued cooperation, but it altered the tone of the relationship and contributed to a more guarded view of Volisanian authority.

== Maritime exploration ==

The war also encouraged outward-looking activity. Sarta began sending ships on exploratory voyages during this period, partly in search of trade, partly in search of strategic knowledge, and partly as a response to the pressures of war. These voyages did not immediately produce a new overseas order, but they marked an important change in Sartan seafaring.

Exploration, shipbuilding, and coastal trade became more closely linked to state policy. In later Sartan writing, the sea voyages of this period were often presented as evidence that the kingdom had moved beyond simple frontier warfare and into a broader regional role.

== Convention of Paga ==

The [[Convention of Paga]] in 1530 marked the end of the era. It settled the main phase of the long conflict and formalised a broader regional agreement involving Sarta, [[Volisania]], [[Kingdom of Jacan|Jacan]], and the Zamarran states. By that point, the war had already changed Sarta's institutions, social structure, and foreign relations.

The convention did not erase the effects of the previous two centuries. Instead, it closed a long period of warfare and left behind a kingdom that was more institutionalised, more urban, and more self-conscious about its place in Illypnia.

